<resources>

    <!-- Base application theme.

         DayNight on purpose, although every screen the app draws is light and
         the status-bar icons are dark to match (App.tsx, ENG-613). With the
         accessibility force invert dark theme in effect (the secure setting
         accessibility_force_invert_color_enabled under the system dark theme;
         its flag is live on the Pixel 8 Pro under Android 17),
         ViewRootImpl.determineForceDarkType hands a window to HWUI's inversion
         when its theme says isLightTheme=true, barring a per-app override. At
         night a DayNight parent resolves to a dark theme, which says
         isLightTheme=false, so the app is exempt, as it always was. A Light
         parent opts it in, and HWUI inverts a window that renders light, which
         this one does: the white inset under the dark icons, the invoice QR.
         Native widgets styled from this theme (Alert dialogs, for one) follow
         the system theme, as they always have.

         The three window items are the launch state, shown until the
         <StatusBar> in App.tsx applies, and they hold in both system themes.
         Keep them in step with App.tsx, or the icons flip at launch:
         windowBackground is what sits under the icons on Android 15+, where the
         bar is transparent (DayNight alone makes it #303030 at night);
         statusBarColor paints the band on Android 7-14 (ignored on 15+); and
         windowLightStatusBar asks for dark icons on every version.
         __tests__/statusBar.test.tsx checks that they agree. -->
    <style name="AppTheme" parent="Theme.AppCompat.DayNight.NoActionBar">
        <!-- Customize your theme here. -->
        <item name="android:editTextBackground">@drawable/rn_edit_text_material</item>
        <item name="android:windowBackground">@android:color/white</item>
        <item name="android:statusBarColor">@android:color/white</item>
        <item name="android:windowLightStatusBar">true</item>
    </style>

</resources>
